Falsifies `machine_discovery::acpi::verification::the_madt_walk_terminates_and_stays_inside_the_body`.

The defect: the minimum entry length drops from two to one. A MADT entry is
`[type, length, ...]`, so an entry claiming a length of one advances the cursor by
one byte and the next iteration reads its *length* byte as a type and its own
successor's type as a length. The walk no longer consumes at least its own header
per step, which is the only thing making it finite, and a table of `01 01 01 ...`
walks one byte at a time through whatever follows the MADT in memory.

Zero is still refused, which is what makes this the plausible mistake rather than
an obviously broken one: "a zero length would loop forever" is the comment beside
the check, and `len < 1` satisfies that comment exactly. Every test in this file
stays green, because the only malformed lengths they write are zero and 200.

Expected to fail: `assert!(it.at >= last + 2)`, and with a deeper unwind the
`seen <= N / 2` bound behind it.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
index d4fdb7cf..620da25b 100644
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
@@ -330,7 +330,7 @@ impl Iterator for MadtEntries<'_> {
         // A zero (or absurdly short) length is a malformed table, and continuing would either loop
         // forever or read the next entry from the middle of this one. Stop, which reports the
         // entries read so far rather than inventing more.
-        if len < 2 || self.at + len > self.body.len() {
+        if len < 1 || self.at + len > self.body.len() {
             return None;
         }
         let e = &self.body[self.at..self.at + len];
